cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Open Door Range
message: >-
  If you use Open Door Range in teaching, research, or an
  assessment, please cite it. The OSDP attacks it teaches are
  the work of Bishop Fox; see the references below and credit
  them too.
type: software
authors:
  - name: LSOH
    website: "https://github.com/holdTheDoorHoid"
repository-code: "https://github.com/holdTheDoorHoid/open-door-range"
url: "https://holdthedoorhoid.github.io/open-door-range/"
abstract: >-
  A browser-based virtual range for physical access control.
  It simulates the path from credential to reader to controller
  to door — Wiegand and clock-and-data through OSDP Secure
  Channel — so that reader-to-panel attacks can be taught and
  defended against without any hardware. One Rust engine,
  compiled to WebAssembly for the site and to a native tool for
  analysing real captures, so the teaching simulation and the
  analyser are the same code.
keywords:
  - OSDP
  - physical security
  - access control
  - Wiegand
  - RFID
  - security education
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
references:
  - type: conference-paper
    title: "Badge of Shame: Breaking into Secure Facilities with OSDP"
    authors:
      - family-names: Petro
        given-names: Dan
      - family-names: Vargas
        given-names: David
    year: 2023
    notes: >-
      Presented at Black Hat USA and DEF CON 31. The five OSDP
      attacks this project teaches are from this research; the
      accompanying tool is BishopFox/mellon.
  - type: standard
    title: "SIA OSDP (Open Supervised Device Protocol), v2.2.2"
    year: 2024
    institution:
      name: Security Industry Association
